# Flashing Junkyard Debian onto Pixel Fold

This folder is everything needed to flash a batch of Pixel Folds. Work through
the steps in order. **Everything you need is in here — do not download anything.**

This kit builds:

| | |
|---|---|
| Image version | `@IMAGE_VERSION@` |
| Kernel version | `@KERNEL_VERSION@` |
| Google build | `@FELIX_BUILD@` (bundled under `factory/`) |
| Packaged | @PACKAGED_DATE@ |

**What you need**

- A Linux machine with `adb` and `fastboot` (`platform-tools` from Google;
  `fastboot` must be 33.0.1 or newer).
- A **powered** USB hub. `super.img` is 7.9 GB per phone, so a batch of eight is
  ~63 GB through one USB controller. An unpowered hub will drop phones mid-flash.
- ~50 GB free disk space.

**Check the kit arrived intact before you start:**

```
sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS
```

---

## Step 1 — Prepare each phone

On every phone, in Android:

1. Settings → About phone → tap **Build number** seven times.
2. Settings → System → Developer options → enable **OEM unlocking** and
   **USB debugging**.

Plug all the phones into the hub.

## Step 2 — Get them into fastboot

```
./reboot-to-fastboot.sh
```

Phones you have not used before show an **"Allow USB debugging?"** dialog.
Accept it on each, then run the script again — it names any phone still stuck as
`unauthorized` rather than skipping it quietly.

Wait until every phone shows the fastboot screen.

## Step 3 — Unlock the bootloaders

```
./unlock-bootloaders.sh
```

⚠ Each phone shows a confirmation you must accept with the **volume and power
buttons**. This cannot be automated.

⚠ Unlocking **factory resets** the phone. That is expected.

## Step 4 — Flash Google's image

```
./flash-google-batch.sh
```

This runs Google's own `flash-all.sh` (already unpacked in `factory/`) on every
attached phone at once. Nothing to download and nothing to unzip.

This step is not optional and it is not the same as step 5. It installs the
bootloader and radio firmware, which our image never touches.

⚠ **Do not substitute a different Google build.** Our system carries hardware
firmware taken from build `@FELIX_BUILD@` and it has to match the bootloader and
radio installed here. A mismatch does not fail cleanly — it looks like our
software is broken.

⚠ This **wipes** each phone, and reboots it several times on its own. Do not
unplug anything until the summary appears. Each phone writes `google-<serial>.log`.

Afterwards the phones boot into stock Android. Leave them at the welcome screen
and put them back into fastboot: hold **volume down + power** while the phone
restarts.

## Step 5 — Flash the Junkyard image

With every phone in fastboot:

```
./flash-batch.sh
```

This flashes all attached phones at once and prints `OK` or `FAILED` per phone.
Each phone also writes its own `flash-<serial>.log`.

⚠ **Do not unplug anything until the summary appears.** 7.9 GB per phone is
slow. Slow is normal; slow is not stuck.

If a phone fails, re-run just that one — you do not need to redo the batch:

```
./flash-fastboot.sh <serial>
```

The phones reboot themselves when finished.

## Step 6 — Check the screens

**First boot takes up to 90 seconds.** A phone on the Google logo for a minute
and a half is still working. Only treat it as stuck after three minutes.

When it finishes, each phone shows a login screen on its own display. **Read it.**
It should look like this:

```
Debian GNU/Linux 13 tty1

Image:  @IMAGE_VERSION@  (built @IMAGE_BUILD_DATE@)
Kernel: @KERNEL_VERSION@
Battery: 87%
IPv4:   10.42.0.1
Boot:   slot A
```

Check these three lines on every phone:

| Line | Must read | If it does not |
|---|---|---|
| `Image:` | `@IMAGE_VERSION@` | Re-flash: `./flash-fastboot.sh <serial>` |
| `Kernel:` | `@KERNEL_VERSION@` | Re-flash: `./flash-fastboot.sh <serial>` |
| `Boot:` | `slot A` | Fine if it says `slot B` — see "About slots" |

`Battery:` and `IPv4:` will differ per phone and do not matter here.

**Why read the screen rather than trust the flash.** A phone can accept a flash,
report success, and still boot something else — that is the specific failure this
check exists to catch, and the screen is the only place it shows. A phone that
displays the two lines above is running what you flashed.

**A phone showing no login screen at all after three minutes** has not come up.
Re-flash it. If it fails twice, set it aside and report the serial.

---

## About slots

Each phone keeps two copies of the system, called slot A and slot B. This kit
puts the **same image in both**, so a phone is fine whichever slot it starts on.

If `Boot:` reads `slot B` instead of `slot A`, the phone chose its backup copy.
It is running the same software and needs nothing done to it. Worth mentioning
in your notes if it happens on many phones, but it is not a fault.

### Nothing to do about slots here

A freshly flashed phone is on probation: it has a limited number of start-ups to
prove itself before falling back to its other copy. It leaves probation once it
reaches a working network, which normally happens where the phones are finally
installed — not here.

**This does not need managing during flashing.** Both copies hold the same
image, so a phone that falls back is running exactly the same software. Powering
phones on and off a handful of times while you check screens is well within
budget.

Just avoid leaving a phone power-cycling repeatedly — dozens of times — before it
has ever been on a network. Normal handling is fine.

---

## If something goes wrong

**A phone is missing from `fastboot devices`.** It is not in fastboot, or the
cable is charge-only. Try another cable first — this is the most common cause.

**`flash-fastboot.sh` says "not in fastboot — skipping".** That phone booted
while the batch ran. Put it back in fastboot and re-run it for that serial.

**A phone reboots by itself during flashing.** Re-flash it. Tell us if the same
phone does it more than once.

**Anything you set aside:** send us the serial number and what its screen showed
(a photo is ideal). The serial is on the fastboot screen and on the box.
